Let me explain the mechanism carefully, because the "why" is what separates memorising advice from understanding it.

A database primary-key index is a B-tree sorted by the key. When the key is monotonically increasing (1, 2, 3, ... or a time-ordered id), every new row's key is larger than all existing keys, so it appends to the RIGHTMOST leaf page. That page stays hot in memory, fills up neatly, and when full a new page is added at the end — no existing data moves. This is the efficient case.

A random UUIDv4 has no order. A newly generated value like `3f2a...` might sort before half the existing keys and after the other half, so it must be inserted into the MIDDLE of the B-tree. If the target leaf page is already full, the database performs a PAGE SPLIT: it allocates a new page and moves roughly half the rows across to make room. Page splits are expensive (extra I/O and locking) and leave both pages only about half-full, which is INDEX FRAGMENTATION — the index occupies far more pages than the data needs, wasting disk and memory.

The second cost is CACHE/BUFFER-POOL locality. With sequential keys, inserts hammer the same few rightmost pages, which stay in the buffer pool, so inserts rarely hit disk. With random keys, each insert targets a different page scattered across the index; those pages must be read from disk into the buffer pool first, evicting others, so a table larger than memory thrashes. Insert throughput collapses as the table grows beyond the cache.

```
  Sequential/time-ordered keys          Random UUIDv4 keys
  ┌──┬──┬──┬──┐  append →               ┌──┬──┬──┬──┐  insert into MIDDLE
  │..│..│..│  │← new row                │..│XX│..│..│← page full → SPLIT!
  └──┴──┴──┴──┘                          └──┴──┴──┴──┘  half-full pages everywhere
  one hot page, no splits                many pages touched, fragmentation
```

Why it is WORSE on MySQL InnoDB: InnoDB uses a CLUSTERED index, meaning the primary key IS the physical ordering of the table rows themselves — the row data lives in the leaf pages of the primary-key B-tree. So a random primary key scatters the ACTUAL ROWS, not merely a separate index. Two extra penalties follow. First, every SECONDARY index in InnoDB stores the full primary key as its row pointer, so a 16-byte random UUID makes every secondary index bigger and slower than a 8-byte bigint would. Second, the clustered scatter means range scans and inserts both suffer. PostgreSQL uses heap tables with separate indexes, so the damage is contained to the index rather than the row storage, but random UUID indexes still fragment and still thrash the cache.

The fix is a TIME-ORDERED identifier so inserts append near the end while keeping global uniqueness:
- UUIDv7 encodes a millisecond timestamp in the high bits followed by random bits, so values generated over time sort in roughly insertion order.
- ULID is a 128-bit, lexicographically sortable, timestamp-prefixed id with a compact base-32 text form.
- A plain sequential bigint from a sequence is the smallest and fastest when you do not need application-side or distributed generation.

```java
// Problem: random UUIDv4 PK (scatters inserts)
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)   // random v4 → fragmentation
private java.util.UUID id;

// Fix A: time-ordered UUIDv7 (Hibernate 6.2+ style generator)
@Id @org.hibernate.annotations.UuidGenerator(style = org.hibernate.annotations.UuidGenerator.Style.TIME)
private java.util.UUID id;                              // time-ordered → appends

// Fix B: sequential bigint when distribution is not required
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "txn_seq")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "txn_seq", sequenceName = "txn_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;
```

A senior nuance: if you genuinely need a UUID (for example to generate ids on distributed nodes without coordination, or to avoid exposing sequential counts externally), use UUIDv7 or ULID, NOT v4, for the clustered/primary key. Another option is to keep a sequential bigint as the internal clustered primary key for storage efficiency and expose a separate random UUID as the external public identifier with its own index — you get fast inserts internally and non-guessable ids externally.

